{
  "result": "Waiting is an underrated skill. In a world calibrated for instant confirmation, we treat delay like a software bug, something to patch as quickly as possible. Yet the empty spaces between intention and fruition are where perception ripens. A seed shoved into a greenhouse under perpetual light may sprout faster, but a tree that grows on a slow hillside develops growth rings that record every drought, every storm. People are no different. The minutes spent in lines, on platforms, at red lights feel like theft, yet they can be small scholarships awarded by the day to study texture. Notice the soft quarrel of two starlings on a wire, the petrichor cooling asphalt, the way your lungs pocket and unfold the air. Gradually, you discover that anticipation has its own flavor, separate from the thing awaited. Musicians know: silence isn\u2019t absence; it is a held breath that magnifies the note to follow. Likewise, deliberate pauses inside ordinary routines let us admire contours we rush past when the signal flips too soon. Embrace the latent interludes. Let them stretch, reshape, and season you; bake complexity into the crust of everyday experience. One day, when something truly matters and you have no choice but to wait, you\u2019ll find patience already familiar, as if it has been sitting beside you all along, nursing a quiet grin. The art of waiting is simply the art of being awake to what unfolds when you thought nothing was happening. In that unnoticed province, life rehearses its miracles.",
